import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { TagsInputHero } from "../../demos/tags-input/hero.rx"
import { TagsInputRules } from "../../demos/tags-input/rules.rx"
import { TagsInputForm } from "../../demos/tags-input/form.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Tags Input" subtitle="A text input that turns what is typed into a list of removable tags.">
    <Demo source="tags-input/hero"><TagsInputHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>
      Import the parts and put them together. The tags are rendered from the <code>tagsInput</code> context, one
      {" "}<code>TagsInputTag</code> per entry of its <code>tags</code>:
    </p>
    <Code file="tags-input/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      There is no native tags input, and Base UI has none either; this family follows the tags inputs of Ark UI,
      Bits UI and Melt UI, with React Aria's TagGroup as the guide for its keys. A plain text{" "}
      <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> does most of the work:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Typing, the caret and selection, a <code>placeholder</code>, and paste from the clipboard.</li>
      <li>IME composition events: the library adds nothing while a composition is in progress.</li>
      <li><kbd>Enter</kbd> in an empty input submits the form, as in any other text field.</li>
      <li>
        Focus. It never leaves the input: a tag is highlighted, not focused, so a <code>FieldLabel</code> names the
        input and the field's touched state follows it.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <code>required</code> constraint: the input is natively <code>required</code> while there is no tag.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The library adds the list: adding an entry on <kbd>Enter</kbd> or the delimiter, splitting what is pasted,
      the rules an entry must pass, the keys that move between the input and the tags and remove them, the remove
      and clear buttons, a polite live region that announces a highlighted or removed tag, and one hidden input per
      tag for the form.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        There is no Label part: put the tags input in a <code>FieldRoot</code> with a <code>FieldLabel</code>, as the
        demo above does, or give the input an <code>aria-label</code> or a <code>&lt;label for&gt;</code>. Editing a
        tag in place (a double-click in Ark UI) isn't supported: remove it and type it again.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="keyboard">Keyboard</h3>
    <p>
      In the input, <kbd>Enter</kbd> adds what was typed, trimmed, and so does typing the <code>delimiter</code>
      {" "}(a comma unless you pass another, or <code>null</code> for none). <kbd>Backspace</kbd> or{" "}
      <kbd>ArrowLeft</kbd> with the caret at the start of the text highlights the last tag. With a tag
      highlighted, <kbd>ArrowLeft</kbd> and <kbd>ArrowRight</kbd> move the highlight (past the last tag, back to
      the text), <kbd>Home</kbd> goes to the first tag, <kbd>End</kbd> and <kbd>Escape</kbd> back to the text,
      {" "}<kbd>Backspace</kbd> removes the tag and highlights the one before it, and <kbd>Delete</kbd> removes it and
      highlights the one after. Typing drops the highlight. Otherwise the keys do what they do in any text input.
    </p>
    <p>
      Style a highlighted tag with <code>[data-highlighted]</code>. Since focus stays in the input, put the focus
      ring on <code>TagsInputControl</code> with <code>:focus-within</code>. A press on a tag highlights it, a press
      on the control's empty space puts the caret in the input, and the remove buttons and the clear button work
      without taking focus from the input, so none of them is in the tab order.
    </p>

    <h3 id="rules">Rules for an entry</h3>
    <p>
      An entry is refused when it is already a tag (unless <code>allowDuplicates</code>), when there are{" "}
      <code>max</code> tags, or when <code>validate</code> returns false for it. A refused entry stays in the input
      to be corrected, and <code>onValueInvalid</code> gets <code>{"{ value, reason }"}</code>, with the reason{" "}
      <code>duplicate</code>, <code>max</code> or <code>invalid</code>, to show a message.
    </p>
    <p>
      With <code>addOnPaste</code>, everything pasted is added at once, split at the delimiter and at line breaks;
      without it, a paste is treated like typing, so the parts before a delimiter are added and the rest stays in
      the input. <code>addOnBlur</code> adds what was typed when the input loses focus. The demo below takes up to
      five email addresses separated by spaces, and does both.
    </p>
    <Demo source="tags-input/rules"><TagsInputRules /></Demo>

    <h3 id="controlled">Controlling it</h3>
    <p>
      <code>value</code> and <code>onValueChange</code> hold the tags, a list of strings; <code>inputValue</code>{" "}
      and <code>onInputValueChange</code> hold the text. The root calls your handlers in a change of their own, so a
      handler that saves to a server never holds the tags or the input while its request is out: write the new
      tags with <code>guess</code> to show them at once, or leave <code>value</code> alone until the server has
      them. A refused save leaves the text empty, as the entry was accepted by the input; keep the text yourself
      (in <code>inputValue</code>) if a failed save should put it back. With <code>disabled</code> while a save is
      out, the input takes focus back when it is enabled again.
    </p>

    <h3 id="forms">Forms</h3>
    <p>
      With a <code>name</code>, or inside a <code>FieldRoot</code> that has one, each tag is submitted with the form
      as its own entry, and a form reset puts the default tags back and empties the input. Inside a field the input
      is labelled and described by it, and the field's value is the list of tags. <code>required</code> means at
      least one tag: the input is natively <code>required</code> while there is none, so text typed but not yet
      added counts as filled, and the form submits without it.
    </p>
    <Demo source="tags-input/form"><TagsInputForm /></Demo>

    <h3 id="announcements">Announcements</h3>
    <p>
      The root renders a visually hidden <code>role="status"</code> region (the only style the family writes) and
      puts a message in it when a tag is highlighted, removed, or all are cleared. The messages are English by
      default; pass <code>messages</code> to change them. Each remove button is named "Remove" and its tag's value;
      give it an <code>aria-label</code> of your own to change that.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="tags-input" />
  </DocsPage>
}
